       "editorialNotes": {
        "short": "The R&B singer celebrates SoCal living on her first album in six years.",
        "standard": "“You guys, so much has happened in the last six years,” <b>Jhené Aiko</b> wrote on social media when she announced her first full-length since 2020’s <i>Chilombo</i>. An hour-long journey around the R&B singer-songwriter’s Southern California homeland—complete with guest appearances by locals like <b>Kendrick Lamar</b> and <b>Ab-Soul</b>—<i>Westside Whimsy</i> is full of luxuriant textures that update quiet storm for the bedroom-pop generation.\n\nAt times, those plush synths serve as a landing spot for anxieties: “I can’t escape the heartbreak,” she muses over fuzz-covered pianos and strings on <b>“Break,”</b> while <b>“Don’t Be That Guy”</b> pairs a striding beat with Aiko gently, kindly affirming her boundaries.\n\nAiko lets the sunshine in on cuts like <b>“Beach Boy,”</b> a gently swaying love song where her voice curls around her wonder at the “good vibrations” she’s feeling, and <b>“So Good,”</b> where she and Lamar steal admiring glances at each other over synths.",
        "tagline": "New Album"
